 You're absolutely right, I let that slip. The dependency is strictly one-way — WindowCoordinator is aware of WindowDispatcher's output, but WindowDispatcher has no reference to WindowCoordinator whatsoever. It produces envelopes and hands them off, full stop. It doesn't consult anything about what's already alive.

That also means single-instance constraint checking doesn't happen inside the dispatcher's dispatch methods directly — it happens before the call, from whoever is orchestrating the dispatch. That's GUIEngine's concern, not the dispatcher's.

---

With that corrected, and with explicit ownership semantics on the return types:

```cpp
class WindowDispatcher
{
public:

    // Registration phase
    WindowFactoryID addWindowFactory(WindowFactory&& factory);

    // Fires the designated startup window on runtime activation
    std::unique_ptr<WindowEnvelope> dispatchStartupWindow();

    // Targeted dispatch by factory ID
    std::unique_ptr<WindowEnvelope> dispatchWindow(WindowFactoryID id);

    // Subsidiary window dispatch - parent instance ID carried into the context
    std::unique_ptr<WindowEnvelope> dispatchSubsidiaryWindow(
        WindowFactoryID id,
        WindowInstanceID parentId
    );

private:

    std::unordered_map<WindowFactoryID, WindowFactory> factories;
    WindowFactoryID nextId{ 0 };
};
```

The `unique_ptr` returns make ownership transfer unambiguous — the dispatcher produces the envelope and immediately relinquishes it. Whoever receives that pointer owns it, which in practice is always WindowCoordinator via GUIEngine's orchestration.

